    "summary": "Seamless He's Gone‑Truckin’ suite with soaring vocals and tight jams",
    "blurb": "The Spectrum show delivers a high‑energy second set that stitches He’s Gone, Truckin’, Spanish Jam and Dark Star into a flawless flow. Jerry’s vocals soar on Here Comes Sunshine and Sing Me Back Home while the band rides Phil’s booming bass and Keith’s crushing keys. A pristine soundboard capture makes this performance a must‑listen.",
    "review": "March 24, 1973 at The Spectrum finds the Dead in top form, launching the evening with bright renditions of Bertha and Here Comes Sunshine before building toward an unforgettable second set. The band strings together He’s Gone → Truckin' → Spanish Jam → Dark Star → Sing Me Back Home with seamless transitions, each song feeding into the next while the musicians explore deep improvisational territory. Jerry Garcia’s voice is both powerful and tender, especially on the heartfelt Sing Me Back Home, and his guitar tone cuts cleanly through Keith Godchaux’s psychedelic fills. Phil Lesh lays down heavy bass bombs that drive the jams, and Bob Weir provides rock‑steady rhythm work and solid harmonies throughout. The dual drumming of Mickey Hart and Billy Kreutzmann locks the groove tight, giving the long Truckin' excursion a propulsive feel. Coupled with an almost flawless Betty soundboard mix—crisp instrument separation, clear vocals, and virtually no dropouts—the show stands out as one of the season’s highlights.",
